(about "parties as kernels, from examples/parties/explore/kernels, 2026-10-19")

(law kernel-reads-no-value
  "A kernel decides by connection, by topic compared by its CE, and by place in its order. It passes V on whole and never reads it. In kernel.nim the admits method is given a verb and a topic, never V, so a kernel built on it can't read what is said.")

(finding {
  kind: party-only
  open: {arrived admits into by record left}
  fixed: {naming ordering letting-go}
  why: "only the party sees every connection, every hearer of a topic, every owner and the one count. Each kernel here overrides one to three of the six and nothing else."
})

(finding {
  kind: participant
  decides: "what a topic or a value means, whom to answer, whether to believe what P claims"
  enforcing: "a rule about meaning that must hold for everyone in a topic is a participant owning the topic. The owner does for one topic what the kernel does for all, in user space."
})

(finding {
  kind: hijack
  where: "src/bl/parties/parties.nim, own"
  case: "eve learns ann's name, connects again saying (own {ANN}) first, and from then on what bob says into ann's name reaches eve alone. ann hears none of it. out/probe-stock.txt"
  smallest: "the party owns each name for whoever goes by it, as it arrives: the one method in sealed.nim. Then (own {ANN}) is refused like any owned topic."
})

(finding {
  kind: eavesdrop
  case: "status tells every name, and (sub NAME) hears what is said into it. eve hears bob's (psst) to ann. out/probe-stock.txt"
  smallest: "the same method as the hijack. With names owned, a say into a name reaches its connection alone."
})

(proposal {
  need: "a program learns what its party gives"
  smallest: "P in (name N S P) is already any value, and the attendee and here.nim pass it through as Said.party. A kernel goes by (KERNEL ARGS ORDER): (sealed 0x…), (gated {status (kitchen) (desk)} 0x…), (fenced TOPICS P-INSIDE). No new value, no change to the dialect."
  leak: "a local reading while programs only compare P and read what they know of it. It becomes a contract once a program refuses a P it can't read, or once P carries a version. The guarantee never comes from the claim. It comes from who this machine lets serve at that place."
})

(finding {
  kind: choosing
  how: "a program chooses a kernel by the value it dials. Several kernels on one machine are several places, each the place of a value. Which value gives what is the program's own knowledge, asked of its own searches, not a directory."
})

(finding {
  kind: order-outlives-process
  kernel: recorder
  case: "served again on its log, the recorder goes by the same P and greets ann at 36, after the 35 it had said. P names an order, not a process. out/probe-recorder-again.txt"
})

(finding {
  kind: layer
  built: "fence.nim"
  gives: "a guarantee in front of any party. Both the fence's guarantee and the inner kernel's hold. A refusal takes an N in the inner order, since the fence says (fenced X) inside, outside the dialect, and the inner party says it back as invalid."
  costs: [
    "two sockets and a copy for each program"
    "names and order are the inner party's, so a new connection inside is a new name outside, and either end closing closes both"
    "a layer limits what its own connections do, never what others do to them. An unfenced program inside can still hear or own a fenced program's name"
    "a layer sees only its own connections, so a recorder as a layer records only them"
  ]
})

(finding {
  kind: composing
  case: "kernels don't stack in one process. Sealed and veiled together is a new type of a few lines. Layers stack by place, each seeing less."
})

(gap {
  need: "admit some programs and not others"
  why: "a name is given on arrival, so there is no list of names to admit before it. Over TCP on ::1 the party sees nothing of who connected."
  smallest: "let the machine decide: a party at a place only some programs' sandbox profile reaches. The profile is the admission list. Anything finer needs a first value, which changes the dialect."
})

(finding {
  kind: sandbox
  case: "values guest under sandbox-exec, reaching only the fence's port, is served by a cook attending the party inside. bl party to the party inside from the same sandbox fails with Operation not permitted. out/sandbox.txt"
  halves: ["the OS decides which places a program reaches" "the party at that place decides what it does there"]
  fence-rule: "a fenced program says into admitted topics and into the names it has heard from. It can answer, and can't seek anyone out."
})

(gap {
  need: "attend a party served at Port(0) without rebuilding"
  why: "here.nim takes its port from -d:partyPort alone, so values is rebuilt for every kernel run here"
  smallest: "BL_PARTY taking [ADDR]:PORT as bl party's --at does"
})

(conversations {
  back: [
    "the rule: the six methods are a conversation's rule, cut by when each applies"
    "admits, now by topic"
    "names: S as nameFor, nil when the party says something itself"
    "kick: leave"
    "joined and left: (alive S) and (dead S) in status"
    "one message to everyone before the next: one N for each say"
    "keep: the recorder keeps a value before anyone is told it, as a conversation kept before passing on"
    "the hold split: nothing a participant holds lets it act as the party, since the split is now the socket"
  ]
  gone: [
    "transactions, and refuse undoing what was kept"
    "all and first in one process. Their place is layers: a fence in front of a recorder is both"
    "participants as procs in the process: every participant is a connection"
    "the speaker hearing its own say"
  ]
})
